[2026-07-22T21:02:46.376Z] [INFO]         "text": "I have all the material needed. Here are my findings.\n\n---\n\n# CI/CD Reference Material for link-assistant/formal-ai (issue #828)\n\n## 1. CI/CD Best-Practices Doc (hive-mind/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md)\n\n**Section headings:**\n1. Why CI/CD Matters for AI Development\n2. Recommended CI/CD Templates\n3. Key CI/CD Principles — Run Checks Only on Relevant File Changes; File Size Limits; Automated Code Formatting; Static Analysis & Linting; Fast-Fail Job Ordering; Changeset-Based Versioning; Validate the Actual Merge Result; Pre-commit Hooks; Release Automation; Concurrency Control; Secrets Detection; Documentation Validation\n4. Quality Enforcement Strategy\n5. Getting Started\n6. Automatic CI/CD Remediation\n\n**Key rules (direct quotes where important):**\n- **Fast-fail ordering:** Run \"fast checks (~7-30s each)\" (compile, lint, file-size) before \"slow checks (~1-10 min each)\" (full test suites) to get rapid feedback.\n- **Concurrency control:** Group runs and cancel superseded ones — `cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' }}` so the latest main-branch release wins.\n- **File size limit:** \"Enforce a maximum of 1000-1500 lines per code file\" (keeps files within AI context windows).\n- **Warnings as errors:** Static analysis treats violations as failures, e.g. C# \".NET analyzers (warnings as errors)\", Java \"SpotBugs (maximum effort)\".\n- **Validate the actual merge result (determinism):** \"CI must test what will actually be merged, not a stale PR snapshot\" — fetch the base branch and perform a fresh merge before running checks.\n- **Secrets detection:** \"Fail CI immediately if secrets are detected\"; never log sensitive values.\n\n**Not covered by the doc:** flaky-test handling, test isolation, false positives/negatives, retries, per-job timeouts, caching, and matrix builds are NOT addressed as explicit principles. (These appear only as concrete implementation choices in the templates below.)\n\n## 2. Template Workflow File Listings\n\n**link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template** (`.github/workflows/`):\n- `release.yml` (single combined CI/CD Pipeline workflow — there is no separate ci.yml)\n\n**link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template**:\n- `example-app.yml`\n- `links.yml`\n- `release.yml`\n\n**link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template**:\n- `docs.yml`\n- `release.yml`\n\n## 3. Rust Template `release.yml` — Observed Settings\n\nThis one file `release.yml` is the main CI (named `CI/CD Pipeline`), triggered on push to main, PRs, and workflow_dispatch. It sets least-privilege `permissions: contents: read` at the top.\n\n**Clippy / warnings-as-errors:**\n- Global `env: RUSTFLAGS: -Dwarnings` — every compile/clippy step treats warnings as hard errors.\n- Clippy invocation (lint job): `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features` (relies on the global `-Dwarnings`; no explicit `-- -D warnings` flag on the clippy line itself).\n- Also runs `cargo fmt --all -- --check`.\n- Rustdoc gate: `cargo doc --no-deps --all-features` with `RUSTDOCFLAGS: -D warnings` (comment notes rustdoc-only lints aren't caught by clippy or doctests, so this is a fail-closed gate before release).\n\n**Test determinism / flakiness handling:**\n- No test retries, no `--test-threads`, no cargo-nextest. Tests run via `cargo test --all-features --verbose` plus `cargo test --doc --verbose`.\n- Determinism is enforced upstream instead: a dedicated **Cargo.lock Guard** job (`check-cargo-lock.rs`) requires binary crates to commit `Cargo.lock` so \"CI and release jobs resolve the same dependency graph every run\" (also prevents cache-key degradation to an empty hash).\n- Network flakiness is mitigated via env: `CARGO_NET_RETRY: '10'` and `CARGO_HTTP_MULTIPLEXING: 'false'` (comment: \"Harden Cargo registry index and crate downloads against transient network flakes on GitHub-hosted runners, including curl HTTP/2 framing failures\").\n\n**Concurrency groups:** Yes —\n```\nconcurrency:\n  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.ref != 'refs/heads/main' }}\n```\n(Note: this is the inverse form of the doc's example — non-main refs cancel in progress, while main runs are NOT cancelled so releases complete.)\n\n**Timeouts on jobs:** Every job sets `timeout-minutes`. Observed values: detect-changes 5, cargo-lock 5, changelog check 10, lint 10, test 20, coverage 15, and release/build/docker jobs 30-60 (docker build 60). No job runs without a timeout.\n\n**Retry logic:** No step-level or job-level retry actions. The only retry is `CARGO_NET_RETRY: '10'` for cargo network operations.\n\n**Caching:** Uses `actions/cache@v5` for `~/.cargo/registry`, `~/.cargo/git`, and `target`, keyed on `hashFiles('**/Cargo.lock')` with `restore-keys` fallbacks (separate cache keys per job: `-cargo-lint-`, `-cargo-test-`; Windows caches registry only, not `target`). Docker build uses GitHub Actions cache: `cache-from: type=gha`, `cache-to: type=gha,mode=max`.\n\n**Matrix builds:** The `test` job uses `strategy: fail-fast: false` with `matrix: os: [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest, windows-latest]` — cross-OS testing, and `fail-fast: false` so one OS failure doesn't cancel the others.\n\n**Change-based gating (fast-fail):** A `detect-changes` job computes outputs (`rs-changed`, `toml-changed`, `docs-changed`, `workflow-changed`, `any-code-changed`); downstream lint/test/coverage jobs run only when relevant files changed (or on push/workflow_dispatch). Uses the `!cancelled()` guard pattern to keep jobs running on workflow_dispatch when detect-changes is skipped (per actions/runner#491). Lint was intentionally decoupled from the changelog check (referencing hive-mind PR #1024) so it always runs as a fast check."
