[2026-07-22T21:16:23.771Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user assigned me to solve GitHub issue #828 in `link-assistant/formal-ai` (titled \"Check for all false positives, false negatives, warnings and errors in CI/CD and fix them all\"). Explicit sub-requirements from the task prompt:\n   - Download all CI/CD logs and collect data related to the issue into the `./dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829` folder.\n   - Do deep analysis (search online for additional facts), reconstruct the timeline/sequence of events, list each and every requirement from the issue, find the root cause of each problem, and propose possible solutions/plans for each requirement.\n   - Check online for known existing components/libraries that solve similar problems.\n   - If there is not enough data to find the actual root cause, add debug output and a verbose mode (default OFF) so root cause can be found next iteration.\n   - If the issue is related to another repo/project, report issues on GitHub for that project with reproducible examples, workarounds, and fix suggestions.\n   - Double-check requirements are fully applied to the ENTIRE codebase: if an issue exists in multiple places, apply the fix in ALL of them.\n   Issue #828 itself requires: fix the failing default-branch CI/CD run; compare against three pipeline templates (link-foundation/rust-, js-, python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template) and adopt best practices (file upstream issue if same problem exists in a template); follow hive-mind CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md; do everything in the single PR #829.\n   Work must be on branch `issue-828-4962df13517a`, updating existing PR #829. Push only to that branch.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - GitHub Actions CI/CD (release.yml with ~17 jobs; desktop-release.yml). Failing job: \"E2E Tests (agent CLI ↔ formal-ai)\".\n   - formal-ai: a Rust symbolic/deterministic AI assistant exposing OpenAI/Anthropic/Gemini-compatible HTTP APIs; it is a LEARNING system with persistent shared memory.\n   - Agent CLI E2E tests: bash scripts in `experiments/agent_cli_e2e/` that run the `agent` (@link-assistant/agent v0.25.0) and `opencode` (v1.18.4) CLIs against a locally-served formal-ai binary, using a mock MCP server for websearch/webfetch.\n   - Shared memory contamination: server reads/writes `$HOME/.formal-ai/memory.lino` and feeds it into deterministic tool planning; `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_PATH` env overrides the path; `FORMAL_AI_RECORD_CHAT` (default on) controls recording; `FORMAL_AI_DREAMING=0` disables background memory-compaction thread.\n   - Flaky test / false negative diagnosis: chat-round count as a weak proxy assertion.\n   - Client-side agentic nondeterminism: opencode chains a variable number of webfetch tool calls.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `experiments/agent_cli_e2e/run_issue_687.sh` (THE failing test; EDITED):\n     - Original failing assertion: `posts=\"$(grep -c 'POST /v1/chat/completions' \"$LOG\" || true)\"; [ \"$posts\" -ge 11 ] || fail \"expected at least 11 chat rounds, got $posts\"`. Strong behavioral asserts (kept): gh executed against `link-assistant/formal-ai`, election recalled, `999999` issue URL surfaced, `searches >= 2` websearches.\n     - Edit 1 — memory isolation on serve (added before `\"$BIN\" serve`): `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_PATH=\"$WORKDIR/memory.lino\" FORMAL_AI_DREAMING=0` with explanatory comment about cross-test contamination (issue #828). `$WORKDIR` is already `mktemp -d`, cleaned on EXIT trap.\n     - Edit 2 — per-turn diagnostics (R6): added `posts_so_far()` helper, `ROUND_TRACE=\"${ROUND_TRACE:-1}\"`, `ROUND_BREAKDOWN`, `prev_posts=0`; in `run_turn()` compute `turn_posts=$((now_posts - prev_posts))`, append to ROUND_BREAKDOWN, print `-> $label used $turn_posts chat round(s), $now_posts total`.\n     - Edit 3 — failure path: wrapped the `>= 11` check to print `== per-turn chat-round breakdown ==` + ROUND_BREAKDOWN before failing.\n     - Edit 4 — success path: after the OK echo, print the breakdown too.\n   - `experiments/agent_cli_e2e/run_issue_771.sh`, `run_agent_cli.sh`, `run_issue_822.sh`, `run_issue_715_opencode.sh` (EDITED): added `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_PATH=\"$WORKDIR/memory.lino\" FORMAL_AI_DREAMING=0` (715_opencode uses `$WORK`; 822 keeps its existing FORMAL_AI_DIALOG_LOG_DIR) with the same isolation comment. These are the scripts that count POST rounds (`grep -c 'POST /v1/chat/completions'`).\n   - `src/server.rs` (READ, not edited): line 220-247 chat completions handler does `SyncStore::open()`, `create_chat_completion_with_solver_and_memory(&request, &solver, store.events())`, then `record_exchange_best_effort(...)`. Confirms memory feeds planning and is written back.\n   - `src/memory_sync.rs` (READ): `configured_memory_path()`→`shared_memory_path()`; `chat_recording_enabled()` default-on unless `FORMAL_AI_RECORD_CHAT=0/false/off`.\n   - `src/shared_memory.rs` (READ): `shared_memory_path()` resolves from `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_PATH` then `$HOME`.\n   - `src/dreaming_runtime.rs` (READ): background thread, DEFAULT_IDLE_SECONDS=60, mutates shared memory; disabled via `FORMAL_AI_DREAMING=0`.\n   - `dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829/ANALYSIS.md` (CREATED): full analysis — data inventory, timeline, requirements table (R1-R7), root cause (§4), codebase audit table (§5), proposed solution (§6), templates/best-practices (§7), upstream reports (§8).\n   - `dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829/research/templates-and-best-practices.md` (CREATED): subagent findings on 3 templates + hive-mind doc.\n   - `dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829/ci-logs/` (CREATED): run-29953691265-full.log (26249 lines), run-29953691265-failed.log, 687-failure-excerpt.txt, passing-run-29937886917-e2e687.log.\n   - `dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829/repro/` (CREATED): build.log, 687-fixed-run{1-5}.log.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - CI failure `expected at least 11 chat rounds, got 10`: Root-caused to (a) cross-test shared-memory contamination (run_issue_687.sh didn't isolate FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_PATH, inheriting ~14 prior E2E scripts' recorded exchanges) and (b) genuine opencode-side variable webfetch chaining in the follow_up turn. Fix in progress: memory isolation + (pending) robust threshold + diagnostics.\n   - Large log outputs overflowed tool results: mitigated with sed to strip job/timestamp prefixes and targeted greps.\n   - Discovery that memory isolation alone does NOT fully stabilize the count (runs gave 11,11,12,13,13) — this reshapes the fix toward also adjusting the threshold rather than relying on isolation alone.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   Confirmed the server-side determinism claim but found the input (shared memory) is not fixed. Proved via 5 local reproduction runs that: (1) with isolated memory the count is >= 11 (never the failing 10), so contamination caused the CI dip to 10; (2) the count still varies 11-13 due to follow_up turn's variable webfetch chaining (client-side, not memory; mock MCP verified to have no randomness). Per-turn breakdown: research=4, report=1, report_destination=1, report_context=2, recall=1 (all stable); follow_up=2-4 (varies). Conclusion forming: keep memory isolation + lower the `>= 11` threshold to a robust floor (original was `>= 9`), because behavioral assertions are the real guardrails and `>= 11` was a recent over-tightening.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The only genuine user message was the initial task assignment: \"Download all logs and collect data related about the issue to this repository, and compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829 folder. Use the collected evidence to do a deep analysis (search online for additional facts and data), reconstruct the timeline/sequence of events, list each and every requirement from the issue, find the root cause of each problem, and propose possible solutions and solution plans for each requirement. Also check online for known existing components/libraries that solve a similar problem or can help. If there is not enough data to find the actual root cause, add debug output and a verbose mode (if not already present) so the root cause can be found on the next iteration. Keep the default state switched off. If the issue is related to another repository/project, report issues on GitHub for that project when possible. Each report must contain reproducible examples, workarounds, and suggestions for fixing the issue in code. Double-check that the requirements are fully applied to the entire codebase: if an issue exists in multiple places, apply it in all of them. Proceed.\" (Issue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/828)\n   - A later message was an automated instruction to produce this summary (not a task-changing user request).\n   - Note: A \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY\" message triggered this summary — it is a system/harness instruction, not a new user task.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Finalize the `run_issue_687.sh` threshold decision (lower `>= 11` to a robust floor such as `>= 9`, given the demonstrated 11-13 variance and theoretical dip risk) — the memory isolation + diagnostics are already applied.\n   - Validate the other 4 edited scripts still pass (especially run_issue_715_opencode.sh `>= 8`, run_issue_822.sh `>= 3`) — ideally run them locally with the built binary.\n   - Update ANALYSIS.md §4/§6 to reflect the NEW finding that isolation alone doesn't fully stabilize the count (follow_up client-side nondeterminism) and the threshold adjustment.\n   - Consider whether the CHANGELOG/changelog.d fragment or version bump is needed (repo has changelog.d/ and release automation).\n   - Commit all changes and push to `issue-828-4962df13517a`; update PR #829 title/description; move PR out of draft (`gh pr ready 829`) when done.\n   - Verify no CHANGELOG fragment / version requirements block CI.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   Immediately before this summary, I ran 3 additional clean reproductions (runs 3,4,5) of the fixed `run_issue_687.sh` to characterize the chat-round distribution. Results: run3 total=11 follow_up=2; run4 total=12 follow_up=3; run5 total=13 follow_up=4. Combined with earlier runs 1 (13) and 2 (11), this established that with isolated memory the count ranges 11-13, with all variance isolated to the `follow_up` turn (2-4 rounds, from variable webfetch chaining by opencode). This confirmed memory isolation eliminates the contamination-driven dip to 10 but does not make the count perfectly deterministic, so the `>= 11` assertion remains fragile at its boundary. I was on the verge of deciding to lower the threshold to a robust floor while keeping isolation and the strong behavioral assertions.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Edit `run_issue_687.sh` to lower the `[ \"$posts\" -ge 11 ]` assertion to a robust floor (the battle-tested original `>= 9`, which the observed clean-run minimum of 11 comfortably clears while still catching a genuinely broken workflow ~6 rounds), updating its failure message accordingly, then re-run the fixed script to confirm it passes, and update `dev/log/issues/828/pulls/829/ANALYSIS.md` §4/§6 to document that isolation removes the contamination-driven dip to 10 but the residual 11-13 variance comes from opencode's client-side webfetch chaining in the follow_up turn. This directly continues the task I was working on (finalizing the root-cause-aware fix for the failing `expected at least 11 chat rounds, got 10` assertion). Verbatim from my most recent reasoning: \"keep memory isolation (removes real contamination cause) + lower the threshold to a safe floor that the clean workflow reliably clears while still catching real regressions\" and \"the original was 9, and it was tightened to 11 in commit 75341798 when turns went 4→6\". I will confirm this threshold choice is consistent with the behavioral guardrails before committing.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/box/.claude/projects/-tmp-gh-issue-solver-1784753385026/d76d13c2-6660-447b-84d5-ad48d489a436.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."
